data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11 (shipped with .NET 11), the **sync-over-async opt-in for the Cosmos DB provider is removed entirely**. The synchronous LINQ operators — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, and `SaveChanges()` — will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime regardless of configuration.

The Cosmos DB provider never had true sync I/O; it was always wrapping async calls. The opt-in (`EnableSyncOverAsync()` or the `CosmosOptionsExtension`) kept that shim alive temporarily, but .NET 11 drops it as a breaking change.

## The fix: go fully async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Summary of required changes:**

| Sync call | Async replacement |
|-----------|-------------------|
| `ToList()` | `await ToListAsync()` |
| `First()` | `await FirstAsync()` |
| `Count()` | `await CountAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `await SaveChangesAsync()` |

You'll also need to propagate `async/await` up through your controllers/services (the "async all the way" pattern). Remove any `EnableSyncOverAsync` configuration — it's no longer recognized.
